    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Libertarians ground their arguments in Lockean self-ownership and non-aggression principles, asserting that legitimate state power cannot exceed the rights to life, liberty, and property, rendering most redistribution inherently coercive. Social democrats rely instead on Kantian human dignity and Rawlsian fairness, arguing that a perpetual commitment to justice requires a safety net that prevents the \"waste of potential\" inherent in a pure competition system. To fully engage with the logic, you must explain how libertarians view inequality as a necessary byproduct of voluntary choice among virtuous individuals, whereas social democrats view it as an avoidable tragedy stem from unthinking market failures. Both sides remain internally consistent because each starts from a different, equally plausible axiom about the nature of personhood and society.",
